{"word": "telegram", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "telegram", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈtɛləˌɡɹæm/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈtɛləˌɡɹeə̯m/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈtɛləˌɡɹɛə̯m/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "telegrams", "tags": ["plural"]}, {"word": "telegramme", "tags": ["alternative", "nonstandard"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "A message transmitted by telegraph.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "There is an hour or two, after the passengers have embarked, which is disquieting and fussy.[…]Passengers wander restlessly about or hurry, with futile energy, from place to place. Pushing men hustle each other at the windows of the purser's office, under pretence of expecting letters or despatching telegrams.", "reference": "1915, G[eorge] A. Birmingham [pseudonym; James Owen Hannay], chapter I, in Gossamer, New York, N.Y.: George H. Doran Company, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "On 20 May 1937 when down in Bradford-on-Avon on business, Bulleid received a telegram with the cryptic news: 'Sir Herbert Walker wishes to see you twelve thirty tomorrow—Gresley'.", "reference": "1979 August, Graham Burtenshaw, Michael S. Welch, “O.V.S. Bulleid's SR loco-hauled coaches - 1”, in Railway World, page 394:"}, {"text": "Indians awoke on Monday to find their 162-year-old telegram service rendered obsolete, superseded by SMS, e-mail and Twitter.\nArguably one of the oldest victims of the digital age, telegrams were the fastest communication method from the 19th century.", "reference": "2013 July 15, Monica Sarkar, “The day telegrams came to a final STOP”, in CNN:"}], "synonyms": ["wire", "cable", "telegrapheme"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "af", "name": "Afrikaans"}, "word": "telegram"}, {"language": {"code": "sq", "name": "Albanian"}, "word": "telegram"}, {"language": {"code": "am", "name": "Am